@using System.Globalization
@using MyRestaurant.DataAccess.Menu
@using MyRestaurant.DataAccess.Orders
@using MyRestaurant.DataAccess.Sittings
@using MyRestaurant.Domain.Identifiers
@using MyRestaurant.Domain.LiveUpdates
@using MyRestaurant.Domain.Orders
@using MyRestaurant.WebApplication.Menu
@using MyRestaurant.WebApplication.Orders
@implements IDisposable
@inject IMenuDirectory MenuDirectory
@inject IMenuItemImageDirectory MenuItemPictures
@inject IOrderReadModel OrderReads
@inject IOrderEventLog EventLog
@inject IOrderWorkflow Workflow
@inject ISittingDirectory Sittings
@inject IDomainEventBroadcaster Broadcaster
@inject IIdentifierFactory Identifiers
@inject RestaurantOptions Options
@inject RestaurantTime RestaurantClock

@* The guest ordering surface (TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION §6, §7, §9, §11.1).

   An INTERACTIVE ISLAND inside a static-SSR page, which is the one structural decision in this file
   worth reading twice. /table/{id} is [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting] because the join flow writes the
   grant cookie and issues redirects, and a Blazor circuit can do neither. But everything §11.1 asks for
   below the join — a basket that survives a mis-tap, a Send that reports per-operation reasons, lines
   that re-badge when the kitchen fulfills them, a party total that moves when someone else orders — is
   live. So the page stays static and hosts this component with @@rendermode="InteractiveServer". The
   parent keeps the cookie work; this keeps the circuit.

   It is deliberately given only the two identifiers it needs and re-derives everything else. The person
   comes from the cascading authentication state rather than from a parameter, so the component is
   correct on its own terms and not merely correct because its caller was careful; membership and
   openness are re-read on every pass, which is what makes the §11.1 flip to a settled view happen
   without anyone telling this component that the counter closed the sitting.

   Nothing here enforces §6.5. The transaction does, under the lock, against a menu it re-reads there
   (§6.6). What this surface refuses — an unavailable item, a quantity outside 1–100, a tick on a line
   that is no longer the guest's to remove — it refuses only so the answer arrives at the moment of the
   tap instead of after a round trip. *@

@* One element around the whole island, and the reason is the attributes on it rather than the
   layout. §11.1's hazard is the same one §11.5 has: a surface that never became interactive looks
   exactly like a live one. Prerendering renders the picker, the basket and the totals; what it does
   not render is anything that responds — every control here is an @@onclick, so on a dead island
   "Add to basket" silently does nothing and the first thing anyone learns is that the basket stayed
   empty. §11.10's pair says both halves: data-live that a circuit produced this markup, data-loaded
   that the reads behind it have answered. *@
<div class="order-surface" id="table-order-surface"
     data-live="@IsLiveAttributeValue"
     data-loaded="@IsLoadedAttributeValue">
    @if (!_loaded)
    {
        <p class="lede">Loading your table…</p>
    }
    else if (_person is null)
    {
        @* Only reachable if the cookie expired between the page render and the circuit starting. *@
        <p class="status-error" role="alert">
            Your session has ended. Please <a href="@AccountRoutes.SignIn">sign in</a> again to see your order.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <h2 class="table-heading">Who is here</h2>

        @if (_sitting is not null)
        {
            <p class="lede">
                Open since @RestaurantClock.Time(_sitting.OpenedAt) —
                @_roster.Count @(_roster.Count == 1 ? "person" : "people") at the table.
            </p>
        }

        <ul class="table-roster">
            @foreach (SittingMemberSummary member in _roster)
            {
                <li @key="member.PersonIdentifier">
                    <span class="table-roster-name">@member.RosterName</span>
                    @if (member.PersonIdentifier == Person)
                    {
                        <span class="chip">you</span>
                    }
                    <span class="table-roster-joined">joined @RestaurantClock.Time(member.JoinedAt)</span>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>

        <hr class="table-rule" />

        @if (_sitting is null)
        {
            @* §11.1: "On SittingClosed, the surface flips to a read-only settled-bill view."

               .order-settled-heading beside .table-heading, for the same reason
               .order-line-adjustment and .order-prune-notice got a class: this heading is the one
               positive marker that the flip has happened, and "the second h2 on the surface" is
               also the heading of the live ordering view. Everything else about the settled state
               is an absence — no picker, no Send, no removal ticks — and a wait that keys on
               absence alone is satisfied by a surface that has not loaded yet. No CSS rule stands
               behind the new class; .table-heading still does all the styling. *@
            <h2 class="table-heading order-settled-heading">This sitting has been settled</h2>
            <p class="lede">
                The counter has closed and settled this table, so nothing more can be ordered against it.
                What was ordered is below, and stays on <a href="/table/history">your order history</a>.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <h2 class="table-heading">Your order</h2>

            @if (_sendStatus is { } sendStatus)
            {
                <p class="status-success" role="status">@sendStatus</p>
            }

            @if (_rejection is { Count: > 0 })
            {
                @* §6.5.9 is all-or-nothing at the granularity of the event, so the honest headline is that
                   nothing at all was written — not that "some items failed". *@
                <div class="status-error" role="alert">
                    <p class="order-reject-lede">
                        <strong>Nothing was sent.</strong> A send is accepted or refused as a whole, so your
                        basket is exactly as you left it. Fix these and send again.
                    </p>
                    <ul class="order-reject-list">
                        @foreach (RejectedOperation problem in _rejection)
                        {
                            <li><strong>@problem.What</strong> — @problem.Reason</li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                </div>
            }

            @if (_pruneNotice is { } pruneNotice)
            {
                @* A class of its own, for the same reason .order-party-line-name got one: this is the
                   only sentence on the surface that reports something the surface did *to the guest's
                   basket* without being asked, and there was no way to address it. Three other
                   p.status-error elements live inside this island — the picker's staging refusal, the
                   §6.5.9 rejection panel's, and the expired-session line — so "the error paragraph" was
                   never a way to name this one. No CSS rule stands behind the new class; .status-error
                   still does all the styling, so nothing changes on screen. *@
                <p class="status-error order-prune-notice" role="status">@pruneNotice</p>
            }

            @* ---- staging area (§11.1) ------------------------------------------------------------ *@
            @* THE MENU IS A LIST OF ITEMS, NOT A <select>. Until M6 Slice 39 this was one dropdown with
               every item flattened into it and the price glued onto the label — "Soup of the day — $4.50
               (currently unavailable)" — which is what a menu looks like when the model cannot express
               one. Three things were wrong with it and only the third is cosmetic. A closed <select>
               shows exactly one option, so a guest deciding what to eat had to open a modal list, read a
               line, close it, and open it again to compare; at sixty items that is not a menu, it is a
               lookup. It had nowhere to put `menu_item.description`, which is the column this whole
               enhancement exists for and which arrived in `0004`. And an <option> renders text only — no
               element, no chip, no room for a rating, a review count or a thumbnail — so every future
               fact about an item would have had to be concatenated into that same label.

               So the item choice is a list of cards, each its own button, and choosing one opens a detail
               panel below the list. The panel is the answer to "see a lot more information about that
               item IF SUCH INFORMATION EXISTS": it names what is recorded, and where nothing is recorded
               it says so in a sentence rather than rendering an empty box. Today that is a description, a
               price, an availability line and when the item first appeared; ratings, reviews and images
               are Stages 4 and 5 of docs/MENU_AND_HANDHELD_PLAN.md and each is a new row in the panel
               rather than a rewrite of this markup, which is the point of building it this way now.

               WHY BUTTONS AND aria-pressed RATHER THAN RADIOS. A radio group is the more precise ARIA
               for "choose exactly one", and it was rejected for a concrete reason: Blazor reconciles the
               `checked` ATTRIBUTE and browsers track the checked PROPERTY, so a radio whose state this
               component owns can drift out of step with the DOM in ways that need a browser to observe.
               Every other control on this island is an @@onclick, the pressed state here is rendered from
               `_pickedMenuItemIdentifier` on every pass, and a button carries no DOM state to reconcile.
               A one-of-many toggle set is a slight stretch of aria-pressed and it is the honest trade
               against a radio group whose keyboard semantics this component would have to emulate.

               `data-menu-item` carries the identifier because §16.3's scenarios choose an item by the
               identifier `AdministrationJourneys.CreateMenuItemAsync` handed back, and matching on the
               visible label would make those scenarios fail for a currency setting (the old <option>
               carried the identifier as its value; this attribute is that value's replacement). *@
            <div class="order-picker">
                <h3 class="order-picker-heading" id="order-picker-menu-heading">What would you like?</h3>

                @if (GroupedMenu.Count == 0)
                {
                    @* This sentence used to stand BELOW the picker. A guest reading an empty menu should
                       find the explanation where the list would have been, not underneath a quantity
                       box.

                       Tested against the GROUPED menu rather than against `_menu`, which is not the same
                       question as of 0005: a menu whose every heading is switched off has items and shows
                       none, and a guest looking at a page with a heading-shaped hole in it needs the
                       sentence more than one looking at a genuinely empty menu does. *@
                    <p class="muted order-menu-empty">
                        There is nothing on the menu yet. Please ask a member of staff.
                    </p>
                }
                else
                {
                    <p class="order-picker-lede muted">
                        Tap an item to see what is in it. Nothing reaches the kitchen until you press Send.
                    </p>

                    @* GROUPED UNDER HEADINGS, and this is the half of the enhancement request that needed
                       0005. The loop is an OUTER one around markup that did not change: Slice 39 built the
                       card, and a heading above a run of cards is all that was ever missing.

                       THE WALK ITSELF IS NO LONGER IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS F-100. It was a private
                       property here from Slice 40, which put §11.1's grouping and §7's two
                       opposite-pointing visibility rules somewhere no unit test could reach — there is no
                       bUnit in this repository (§16.1), so the only thing asserting any of it was §16.3
                       scenario 17, which needs a browser and a database. It is now
                       `MenuGrouping.VisibleToGuests`, beside `KitchenQueue` and `OrderStaging` and for the
                       reason those two are outside their components; `MenuGroupingTests` asserts it in
                       milliseconds. What forced the move rather than merely recommending it is that
                       §11.2's "86" panel needs the same walk with the OPPOSITE rule about hidden
                       headings, and a private property cannot be called from a second component.

                       The grouping is done by the DIRECTORY's ordering rather than by a LINQ GroupBy.
                       IMenuDirectory.ListAsync orders by section position, then section name, then section
                       identifier, then the same three for the item — so every item under one heading is
                       contiguous and the walk reads the list once. A GroupBy would work and would quietly
                       re-order the sections into hash order unless it were sorted again, which is the
                       ordering decision being made twice in two files.

                       INACTIVE SECTIONS ARE ABSENT ENTIRELY, and that is §7 rather than an oversight — it
                       is the opposite of the rule one line down for an inactive ITEM, which stays visible
                       and marked. Switching off a heading is a decision about a whole part of the menu
                       ("no breakfast this evening"); 86ing a dish is a decision about one thing a guest is
                       still entitled to know exists. The filtering is in the grouping rather than in the
                       directory because §11.4's administrator must see every heading — and it is which
                       DOOR this surface uses rather than a flag it passes, so the rule is legible from
                       here without reading the other file.

                       THE LOOP VARIABLE IS NOT CALLED `section`, AND THAT IS F-81. `@@section` is Razor's
                       own MVC section directive, so `@@section.MenuSectionName` below was parsed as a
                       directive with a malformed name and never reached C# — the build failed on this
                       line with RZ1011 and said nothing about identifiers. `@@key="section…"` one line up
                       compiled fine, because it does not put the word directly after an `@@`, which is
                       what made the error look like it was about the markup.

                       The body of the inner loop was also four levels shallow, having kept the
                       indentation it had before Slice 40 wrapped it in this outer loop. Corrected in the
                       same pass rather than left, because whitespace is the one thing a reader uses to
                       see which loop a `@@key` belongs to. *@
                    @* The heading index is what decides `loading`, and it is a counter rather than
                       `IndexOf` because the collection is walked once. See `ThumbnailLoading`.

                       NO `@@{` HERE, AND THAT IS F-104. This line is inside the `else` block that opens
                       above, so Razor is already parsing C# — `@@{` is the transition FROM markup INTO
                       code, and writing it where there is no markup to leave is RZ1010: *Unexpected "{"
                       after "@@" character*. A bare statement is what a code block wants. The three other
                       `@@{` blocks in this tree are all correct and all sit directly inside an ELEMENT
                       (`<section>` on /kitchen, `<li>` in the party panel below, `<div>` on §11.4's menu
                       index), which is the difference: the nearest enclosing construct decides whether an
                       `@@` is a transition or a syntax error, and the two shapes look identical on the
                       line itself. *@
                    int headingIndex = -1;

                    @foreach (MenuHeadingGroup menuSection in GroupedMenu)
                    {
                        headingIndex++;

                        <div class="order-menu-section" @key="menuSection.MenuSectionIdentifier">
                            <h4 class="order-menu-section-name" id="@SectionHeadingId(menuSection)">
                                @menuSection.MenuSectionName
                            </h4>

                            @* The heading's own line, which is the last piece of the menu Stage 3 asked
                               for and had deferred: "served until 11am" is the kind of thing it is for
                               (S7). Tested by Length rather than for null, the same rule the item card
                               applies one loop down — the column is NOT NULL DEFAULT '' and "" means
                               "none".

                               Deliberately no aria-describedby on the list below. It would be the more
                               precise ARIA, and expressing "no description means no attribute" needs an
                               attribute whose value is null, which this tree has no precedent for; the
                               honest alternative was rendering the list twice. What is rendered instead
                               puts the sentence between the heading and the list in document order,
                               which is where a screen reader meets it in either case. *@
                            @if (menuSection.MenuSectionDescription.Length > 0)
                            {
                                <p class="order-menu-section-description">@menuSection.MenuSectionDescription</p>
                            }

                            <ul class="order-menu" aria-labelledby="@SectionHeadingId(menuSection)">
                                @foreach (MenuItemSummary item in menuSection.Items)
                                {
                                    bool isChosen = _pickedMenuItemIdentifier == item.MenuItemIdentifier;

                                    @* §7: a deactivated item stays ON the menu, marked, and cannot be
                                       picked — "the guest sees that the salmon exists and is out, rather
                                       than watching it silently vanish". `disabled` is what enforces that
                                       half, and the card keeps its description, because an item a guest
                                       cannot have tonight is still an item they may want to read
                                       about. *@
                                    MenuItemImageMetadata? picture = PictureFor(item);

                                    <li class="order-menu-item @(isChosen ? "is-chosen" : string.Empty) @(item.IsActive ? string.Empty : "is-unavailable") @(picture is null ? string.Empty : "has-picture")"
                                        @key="item.MenuItemIdentifier">
                                        <button type="button" class="order-menu-choice"
                                                data-menu-item="@item.MenuItemIdentifier"
                                                aria-pressed="@(isChosen ? "true" : "false")"
                                                disabled="@(!item.IsActive)"
                                                @onclick="() => ChooseItem(item)">
                                            @* THE TEXT IS ALWAYS WRAPPED, PICTURE OR NO PICTURE, and the
                                               uniformity is deliberate. The card is a grid; with a
                                               thumbnail it is two columns and without one it is one, and
                                               a wrapper that appeared only sometimes would mean two
                                               different row structures for the same rules to place. The
                                               harness reads `span.order-menu-name` and its siblings as
                                               DESCENDANTS of the card, so this wrapper is invisible to
                                               §16.3 scenario 17. *@
                                            <span class="order-menu-body">
                                                <span class="order-menu-line">
                                                    <span class="order-menu-name">@item.Name</span>
                                                    <span class="order-menu-price">@Money(item.PriceAmount)</span>
                                                </span>

                                                @if (item.Description.Length > 0)
                                                {
                                                    @* Tested by Length rather than for null: the column is
                                                       NOT NULL DEFAULT '' and "" means "none" (§7). *@
                                                    <span class="order-menu-description">@item.Description</span>
                                                }

                                                @if (!item.IsActive)
                                                {
                                                    <span class="chip chip-warn">Currently unavailable</span>
                                                }
                                                else if (isChosen)
                                                {
                                                    <span class="chip chip-ok">Chosen</span>
                                                }
                                            </span>

                                            @if (picture is not null)
                                            {
                                                @* THE THUMBNAIL IS LAST IN THE DOCUMENT AND FIRST ON THE
                                                   SCREEN, which is the one thing about this element worth
                                                   reading twice. A button's accessible name is computed
                                                   from its contents in document order, so an <img> placed
                                                   first would make a captioned dish announce as "served on
                                                   a bed of wilted greens, Grilled salmon, £24.00" — the
                                                   description arriving before the thing it describes. It
                                                   is moved into the left column by `grid-column` in
                                                   app.css instead; reordering a NON-interactive element
                                                   visually costs nothing, because there is no focus order
                                                   for it to disagree with.

                                                   `alt` IS ALWAYS WRITTEN AND ITS VALUE IS WHATEVER IS
                                                   STORED, INCLUDING "". That is F-103, and the two are not
                                                   interchangeable: an <img> with NO alt attribute makes a
                                                   screen reader read the URL out, where alt="" makes it
                                                   skip an image whose surroundings already say what it is
                                                   — and here they do, because this button holds the dish's
                                                   name and its price as text. So "" is the RIGHT answer
                                                   for most pictures on this menu, and a caption earns its
                                                   place only when it says something the name does not.
                                                   The plan's own justification for the column had this
                                                   backwards; the column is still worth having, for the
                                                   narrower reason. *@
                                                <img class="order-menu-thumbnail"
                                                     src="@MenuImageRoutes.ForImage(picture.MenuItemImageIdentifier)" alt="@picture.AltText"
                                                     loading="@ThumbnailLoading(headingIndex)"
                                                     decoding="async" />
                                            }
                                        </button>
                                    </li>
                                }
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    }
                }

                @if (PickedMenuItem is { } chosenItem)
                {
                    @* One region, announced politely, so a screen reader hears that choosing an item
                       produced something rather than silently moving focus nowhere. *@
                    <div class="order-menu-detail" id="order-picker-detail" aria-live="polite">
                        <h4 class="order-menu-detail-name">@chosenItem.Name</h4>

                        @if (PictureFor(chosenItem) is { } chosenPicture)
                        {
                            @* §11.1 has said since Slice 39 that this panel is "the surface that item
                               descriptions, and later ratings and images, are read on", so the picture
                               belongs here as much as on the card — and here it is UNCROPPED, which is the
                               reason for having both. The card's thumbnail is a 4rem square under
                               `object-fit: cover`, because bounding a card's height is what keeps a menu
                               scrollable on a 375px screen; a guest who taps a dish to look closer should
                               see the whole photograph.

                               alt="" DELIBERATELY, and it is §11.4's reasoning rather than a copy of the
                               card's. This is the same picture the card already announced, sitting under
                               the dish's own name in the <h4> directly above; a screen reader that read the
                               caption a second time here would be reading one photograph's description
                               twice on one tap. Never loading="lazy": this element renders because
                               somebody just asked for it. *@
                            <img class="order-menu-detail-picture"
                                 src="@MenuImageRoutes.ForImage(chosenPicture.MenuItemImageIdentifier)"
                                 alt=""
                                 decoding="async" />
                        }

                        <dl class="order-menu-facts">
                            <div>
                                <dt>Price</dt>
                                <dd>@Money(chosenItem.PriceAmount)</dd>
                            </div>
                            <div>
                                <dt>Available</dt>
                                <dd>@(chosenItem.IsActive ? "Yes — order away" : "Not right now")</dd>
                            </div>
                            <div>
                                <dt>On the menu since</dt>
                                <dd>@RestaurantClock.Date(chosenItem.CreatedAt)</dd>
                            </div>
                        </dl>

                        @if (chosenItem.Description.Length > 0)
                        {
                            <p class="order-menu-detail-description">@chosenItem.Description</p>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            @* Said plainly rather than left blank. "Nothing else is recorded" is a true
                               and useful sentence; an empty panel reads as a surface that failed to
                               load, which is the same mistake §11.10's data-loaded bit exists to stop
                               one level up. *@
                            <p class="muted order-menu-detail-none">
                                Nothing else has been written down about this one yet. Ask a member of
                                staff if you would like to know more.
                            </p>
                        }
                    </div>
                }

                <div class="form-field order-picker-quantity">
                    <label for="order-picker-quantity">Quantity</label>
                    <input id="order-picker-quantity" type="number" inputmode="numeric"
                           min="@OrderStaging.MinimumQuantity" max="@OrderStaging.MaximumQuantity" step="1"
                           @bind="_pickedQuantity" />
                </div>

                <div class="form-field">
                    <label for="order-picker-note">Anything to note? <span class="optional">(optional)</span></label>
                    <input id="order-picker-note" type="text" maxlength="200" autocomplete="off"
                           placeholder="No onions, extra hot…" @bind="_pickedNote" />
                </div>

                <div class="form-actions">
                    <button type="button" class="button-secondary" @onclick="StageItem">Add to basket</button>
                </div>

                @if (_stagingNotice is { } stagingNotice)
                {
                    <p class="status-error" role="alert">@stagingNotice</p>
                }
            </div>

            @if (_staging.IsEmpty)
            {
                <p class="muted order-basket-empty">
                    Your basket is empty. Pick something above — nothing reaches the kitchen until you press
                    <strong>Send</strong>.
                </p>
            }
            else
            {
                <ul class="order-basket">
                    @foreach (StagedOrderLine staged in _staging.Lines)
                    {
                        MenuItemSummary? item = MenuItem(staged.MenuItemIdentifier);

                        <li class="order-basket-line" @key="staged.StagingIdentifier">
                            <div class="order-line-main">
                                <span class="order-line-name">@(item?.Name ?? staged.MenuItemName)</span>
                                <span class="order-line-price">@Money(StagedLineTotal(staged))</span>
                            </div>

                            @if (staged.CustomizationNote is { } note)
                            {
                                <p class="order-line-note">“@note”</p>
                            }

                            @if (item is null || !item.IsActive)
                            {
                                @* §7: "guest staging areas mark newly-inactive staged items and the send
                                   re-validates server-side regardless". Marked, not silently dropped. *@
                                <p class="order-line-warning">
                                    This became unavailable while it was in your basket. Take it out, or the
                                    whole send will be refused.
                                </p>
                            }

                            <div class="order-basket-controls">
                                <label class="order-basket-quantity">
                                    <span>Quantity</span>
                                    <input type="number" inputmode="numeric"
                                           min="@OrderStaging.MinimumQuantity" max="@OrderStaging.MaximumQuantity" step="1"
                                           value="@staged.Quantity"
                                           @onchange="args => ChangeStagedQuantity(staged.StagingIdentifier, args)" />
                                </label>
                                <button type="button" class="link-button danger"
                                        @onclick="() => Unstage(staged.StagingIdentifier)">
                                    Take out
                                </button>
                            </div>
                        </li>
                    }

                    @foreach (StagedRemoval removal in _staging.Removals)
                    {
                        <li class="order-basket-line is-removal" @key="removal.OrderLineIdentifier">
                            <div class="order-line-main">
                                <span class="order-line-name">Remove @removal.Description</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="order-basket-controls">
                                <button type="button" class="link-button"
                                        @onclick="() => _staging.SetMarkedForRemoval(removal.OrderLineIdentifier, removal.Description, false)">
                                    Keep it after all
                                </button>
                            </div>
                        </li>
                    }
                </ul>
            }

            <div class="form-actions order-send">
                <button type="button" class="button-primary" disabled="@(_staging.IsEmpty || _sending)"
                        @onclick="SendAsync">
                    @(_sending ? "Sending…" : SendLabel)
                </button>
                @if (!_staging.IsEmpty && StagedTotal > 0m)
                {
                    <span class="order-send-total">@Money(StagedTotal) to add</span>
                }
            </div>
        }

        @* ---- the committed living order (§11.1) ---------------------------------------------------- *@
        <h3 class="order-subheading">@(_sitting is null ? "What you ordered" : "Sent to the kitchen")</h3>

        @if (_myLines.Count == 0)
        {
            <p class="muted">You have not sent anything yet.</p>
        }
        else
        {
            <ul class="order-lines">
                @foreach (NarratedOrderLine line in _myLines)
                {
                    <li class="order-line @(line.IsRemoved ? "is-removed" : string.Empty)" @key="line.OrderLineIdentifier">
                        <div class="order-line-main">
                            <span class="order-line-name">@line.Quantity × @MenuItemName(line.MenuItemIdentifier)</span>
                            <span class="order-line-price">@Money(line.Quantity * line.CurrentUnitPriceAmount)</span>
                        </div>

                        @if (line.CustomizationNote is { } note)
                        {
                            <p class="order-line-note">“@note”</p>
                        }

                        <p class="order-line-badges">
                            @if (line.IsRemoved)
                            {
                                <span class="chip chip-warn">Removed</span>
                            }
                            else if (line.IsFulfilled)
                            {
                                <span class="chip chip-ok">At your table</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <span class="chip">With the kitchen</span>
                            }
                            <span class="order-line-when">sent @RestaurantClock.Time(line.AddedAt)</span>
                        </p>

                        @if (line.IsRemoved)
                        {
                            @* §11.1: "removed lines struck-through with actor + reason". *@
                            <p class="order-line-detail">
                                Taken off by @ActorText(line.RemovedByActorRole, line.RemovedByActorPersonIdentifier, Person)@(line.RemovalReason is { } removalReason ? $" — {removalReason}" : string.Empty).
                            </p>
                        }

                        @foreach (NarratedPriceAdjustment adjustment in line.PriceAdjustments)
                        {
                            @* §11.1: "price adjustments shown old → new with reason".

                               .order-line-adjustment beside .order-line-detail, for the same reason
                               .order-prune-notice got a class: the removal sentence directly above
                               uses the identical class, so "the detail paragraph under this line" was
                               never a way to name this one — and this is the sentence §16.3 scenario 9
                               exists to read. No CSS rule stands behind the new class;
                               .order-line-detail still does all the styling, so nothing changes on
                               screen. Same reasoning as Slice 9's .order-party-line-name. *@
                            <p class="order-line-detail order-line-adjustment">
                                Price changed from <s>@Money(adjustment.PreviousUnitPriceAmount)</s>
                                to <strong>@Money(adjustment.NewUnitPriceAmount)</strong>
                                by @DescribeRole(adjustment.ActorRole) — @adjustment.Reason.
                            </p>
                        }

                        @if (_sitting is not null && line.GuestMayRemove(Person))
                        {
                            <label class="order-line-remove">
                                <input type="checkbox"
                                       checked="@_staging.IsMarkedForRemoval(line.OrderLineIdentifier)"
                                       @onchange="args => ToggleRemoval(line, args)" />
                                <span>Take this off my order on the next send</span>
